\title{\textbf{One feedback round for near-optimal quadratic measurement}\\[5pt]
\large Signed spectral compression, an all-noisy separation, and acquired implementations}
\author{Astra P1: central construction with independent internal challenges}
\date{31 October 2026, Australia/Brisbane}
\hypersetup{pdftitle={One feedback round for near-optimal quadratic measurement},pdfauthor={Astra P1},pdfsubject={Quadratic functional prediction, adaptive linear measurements, signed spectral bounds, noise and acquisition}}
\begin{document}\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
For a known quadratic target $f(X)=X^TAX+b^TX+c$ under $X\sim N(0,I)$, an explicit two-round experiment uses at most $k+1$ exact scalar linear observations and has residual squared risk no greater than every unrestricted adaptive $k$-observation policy. The first batch pairs positive and negative eigendirections so their unobserved quadratic compression vanishes; one final observation reads the remaining outcome-dependent linear term. Compression interlacing supplies a matching comparison lower bound, and the expected-budget version follows by convex interpolation. Exact worst-case query complexity is classified by inertia. A separate all-noisy theorem for a normalized Gaussian inner product proves a nonvanishing accuracy advantage against the optimal fixed experiment, including repeated and nonorthogonal directions: at dimension 256 and noise variance 0.01, 12
